{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB01069 - MONDO_5642",
  "verbalization": "Promethazine is suggested to have a potential role in the treatment of atopic conjunctivitis based on its association with papillary conjunctivitis, which in turn is linked to atopic conjunctivitis in multiple pathways. The evidence is supported by the presence of three distinct but overlapping indications: papillary conjunctivitis, tromethamine, and emedastine, all of which are associated with atopic conjunctivitis. These pathways suggest that Promethazine may exert a therapeutic effect through a mechanism that involves the modulation of conjunctival inflammation, possibly through the inhibition of inflammatory mediators or the suppression of immune responses. The shared indication of papillary conjunctivitis across these pathways indicates a potential common biological target or pathway involved in the development of atopic conjunctivitis. The integration of these evidence strands supports the hypothesis that Promethazine could be a candidate for repurposing in the treatment of atopic conjunctivitis, provided further mechanistic validation is conducted to confirm the specific mode of action and its relevance to the disease pathology.",
